On Friday, March 9, 2012 2:42:50 PM UTC-5, Marcus Brubaker wrote:
> Yeah, I'm beginning to see that.  I did a little profiling of my model yesterday and found that about 60% of the time is spent in agrad::chainable::operator new() and the agrad::vari(double) constructor.  I was able to cut that a bit by using a Cholesky decomposition in multi_normal_log (I'll send along this stuff a little later once it's cleaned up) but it still constitutes a huge chunk of computation.

The textbookish multi_normal_log() function is a self-inflicted efficiency disaster, and we had planned to change it. I actually had a branch to fix this when I was using git-svn and stan was using svn on the server. But it was breaking the build for some reason at the time so I couldn't push it and then it got disrupted by the movement of files.

The basic problem is that check_cov_matrix() does a Cholesky decomposition to check whether Sigma is positive definite, but that local computation gets lost when check_positive_definite() exits. So, the L matrix is not available to calculate the determinant or the inverse in the multinormal density.  The cherry on top is that we already construct L in cov_matrix_constrain() and then post-multiply it by L' to get Sigma (to then decompose three times).

It is better to utilize multi_normal_cholesky_log() if possible.

Ben

