Yes, 1 and 2 only apply when Sigma is a parameter. If Sigma is fixed data, we had emailed about inverting Sigma once at the outset (and optionally calculating the determinant) and passing the precision matrix to an appropriately named version of the multivariate normal log density parameterized in terms of the precision matrix. However, nothing came of that discussion yet.

The sequence of calls is in src/stan/prob/transform.hpp: cov_matrix_constrain() does some transformations calls read_cov_matrix(), which calls read_cov_L() to produce L and then returns L * L' . What is needed is a function possibly named L_constrain() that does some transformation and calls read_cov_L() to return L. Then, that L should get passed to mult_normal_cholesky_log() or any other elliptical distribution we may have in the future. Furthermore, if L_constrain() existed, then we could make cov_matrix_constrain() just call L_constrain() and return L * L'.

If you want to do that, go ahead. Otherwise, I'll get to it soon.

Ben

