Another limitation of the way covariance matrices are handled is that we currently do not have a way for the user to utilize the LKJ approach in an intuitive and computationally efficient way. Granted, there are currently only 4 people in the world who think in terms of the LKJ approach and three of them are L, K, and J. But that is the best way to think about it in the stan context.

The Wishart-family approach in stan mostly makes sense from a syntax perspective, e.g.:

master!stan *> cat src/models/basic_distributions/wishart.stan
transformed data {
  cov_matrix(2) S;

  for (i in 1:2)
    for (j in 1:2)
      S[i,j] <- 0.0;

  S[1,1] <- 2.0;
  S[2,2] <- 0.5;
}
parameters {
  cov_matrix(2) W;
}
model {
  W ~ wishart(4, S);
}

The user has a Wishart prior and the Wishart prior implies some unnamed but calculatable prior beliefs about the primitive parameters that are transformed behind the scenes in cov_matrix(). That was a bit weird to program in C++, but is not wrong and not exposed to the user anyway.

But I anticipate users will tend to utilize Wishart-family priors on covariance matrices because that is what they are familiar with in the literature, rather than that is what is entailed by their prior beliefs. The LKJ approach allows the user to have a prior over each standard deviation and a joint prior (usually uniform, which is implied when eta == 1.0) over the correlation matrix.

transformed data {
  real mu(2);
  real(0,) sigma(2);
  real(0,) eta;

  mu[1] <- 0.0;
  mu[2] <- 0.0;
  sigma[1] <- 1.0;
  sigma[2] <- 0.5;
  eta <- 1.0;
}
parameters {
  cov_matrix(2) W;
}
model {
  W ~ lkj_cov(mu, sigma, eta);
}

But lkj_cov_log() is hard-coded to impose a lognormal prior on the standard deviations, which seems unnecessarily limiting. And there is the aforementioned efficiency nightmare where cov_matrix_constrain() first constructs L so that it can be post-multiplied by L' to get W and then lkj_cov_log() conducts a Cholesky decomposition of W to (re)obtain L and then evaluate the density.

To do things the right way, we need a function, perhaps called LKJ_mapping(), that is exposed to the user that takes CPCs and standard deviations and produces L such that W := L * L'. And we need a (trivial) C++ version of lkj_cor_log() that takes CPCs and eta. Then, the .stan file could look something like

parameters {
  real(-1,1) CPCs[K_choose_2];
  real(0,) SDs[K];
}
transformed parameters {
  matrix L(K,K);
  L <- LKJ_mapping(CPCs, SDs);
}
model {
  for(i in 1:K)
    // some prior on SDs[i]

  CPCs ~ lkj_cor(1.0);

  // maybe use L as part of a multinormal likelihood or something
}

Ben

