>     If we want to keep going on optimizing, the way I implemented
>     the compiled Stan data structures is inefficient as it
>     relies on std::vector<std::vector<double>> if you declare
>
>     double x[M,N]
>
>     That's wasteful in storage compared to an Eigen Matrix, which just
>     computes the indexing itself (using the row/col major policy).
>     But we'd need to generalize to x[M,N,K], and so on, to handle
>     all of the Stan types.
>
> Do we just need three dimensional or do we need arbitrary dimensional?

Stan allows as many dimensions as you want.

> If it is the former, note that Eigen supports 3-dimensional Arrays in a
> kludgy fashion by allowing a Matrix or an(other) Array to be the "type"
> for an Array. In other words,
>
> Array< MatrixXd, Dynamic, 1 > x;
>
> is supposed to be a valid declaration.

Sure, but that's going to wash out just like
vector<vector<>> in terms of size (assuming they haven't
built specializations of Array for this case).

You could probably keep going and also have
Array<Array<Array...> but unless they have lots of
template specializations, it'll wash out just like
std::vector<std::vector...> with lots of substructures
redundantly storing length information and pointers
and managing memory.

If you stack them, you can probably use some of the
arithmetic on arrays or matrices, too, because the
template type implements arithmetic.

- Bob

