I think we agree.  I can't even talk about this
stuff coherently.  No wonder legalese has so many
proper names.  What Ben's suggesting license-wise is:

   * R uses GPL.
     This is R's decision and we can't change it.

   * Stan uses BSD.
     Stan only depends on Eigen and Boost,
     which have LGPL and Boost licenses repsectively,
     which I'm pretty sure are compatible with our
     leaving Stan as BSD.

   * rstan uses GPL.
     It depends on both Stan and R.

I think this will work for us.  The important thing is
that rstan acts as an IP firewall the GPL of R and
the BSD of Stan.

I just checked and Google code allows GPL v3 (I seem to
remember them formerly not allowing GPL).  But you only
get a single combobox on the admin page requiring you
to select a single license for the "code".

So let's start a second project for rstan.  It's no
big deal and cleaner in many ways.  We'll probably have
to do the same for MATLAB (god only knows what kind of
proprietary license hell they impose) and Python
interfaces we promised to implement as part of the grant.

I was hoping that rstan could remain BSD and we could
distribute it that way as long as we didn't distribute R or
other GPL-ed code with the understanding that as soon as you
plugged it together with R, it became GPL.  (This is
starting to sound like alchemy.)  But on an umpteenth read,
I think Ben's got the right end of the stick and we'll have
to bite the bullet and separate the package distributions.

- Bob

