https://groups.google.com/d/msg/google-code-hosting/9szR61CySTU/wGdmji0x6EoJ

It seems like the answer is just select GPL for the project and indicate that most of the code is actually BSD and only the R interface is GPL.

>
> So let's start a second project for rstan.  It's no
> big deal and cleaner in many ways.  We'll probably have
> to do the same for MATLAB (god only knows what kind of
> proprietary license hell they impose) and Python
> interfaces we promised to implement as part of the grant.

I'm not sure I see the problem here.  I've never heard anyone complain (or even talk) about issues with using GPL/BSD/etc code in MEX functions.  In fact, there are a large number of, for instance, GPL licensed MEX interfaces out there.  E.G., see http://www.dii.unisi.it/hybrid/tools/mex/downloads.html  Certainly there is no license issues which will impact anything other than the MEX glue code.

>
> I was hoping that rstan could remain BSD and we could
> distribute it that way as long as we didn't distribute R or
> other GPL-ed code with the understanding that as soon as you
> plugged it together with R, it became GPL.  (This is
> starting to sound like alchemy.)  But on an umpteenth read,
> I think Ben's got the right end of the stick and we'll have
> to bite the bullet and separate the package distributions.

I would check with the R project specifically about their external interface.  It may have a different license than the program as a whole.

Cheers,
Marcus

