 > So the licensing issues of the GPL are based on the idea of a derived
 > work.  If you took R, changed it (e.g., forked it) then you have a
 > derived work and it would have to be GPL.  If, on the other hand, you
 > create a GUI which calls R but does not link to R, then you don't have a
 > derived work and the GUI can have any license it chooses.  The issue of
 > plugins can be tricky, but if they're dynamically loaded and not
 > compiled in, then in general they're not considered derived works.
 > However, if you use an (R provided) API that makes it easier to write
 > plugins which is GPL, then it is a derived work and it needs to be GPL
 > as well.  If that GPL plugin calls a library which is BSD, then nothing
 > matters.  In essence, so long as the BSD code doesn't call the R API,
 > then it's fine.  As a result, many projects make plugin APIs LGPL or
 > provide explicit exceptions for plugins and external interfaces.  The
 > Linux kernel is one such example of this.

I've heard this spun both ways.  But it was definitely
what I was thinking about.  So maybe we could've gotten away
with it if we only called the R executables and didn't connect
directly.  But we want to connect directly, and really, we might
