The new auto-dif design is a major change, but compared
to all the other moving parts, not that difficult.  The
issue is whether it'll be worth using more space (up to
twice as much in some cases) to cut down on auto-dif time.
Assuming the extra space doesn't kill us, the time should
be faster despite the increased memory usage.
I'll send out some design notes in a followup e-mail.

The rest of this reply is about what we do now.

Our initial design was based on David Gay's RAD system,
which is now part of the Sacado module of the Trilinos
package.  I found all these papers pretty hard to follow,
but it might help:

   http://www.cs.sandia.gov/~dmgay/ad04_paper.pdf

The Wikipedia page was also helpful.

   http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automatic_differentiation

More comments inline below.

On 3/26/12 4:34 PM, Marcus Brubaker wrote:
> On Sat, Mar 24, 2012 at 4:56 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com
> <mailto:ca...@alias-i.com>> wrote:
>
> One question though is how do you easily mix the autodiff code with hand
> coded derivatives?  It seems like only way to do it is to completely
> overload grad_log_prob or am I missing something?

You need to either

   a.  write your own gradients using grad_log_prob instead
       of grad_log_prob_ad, or

   b.  write gradients for functions and then use those
       as part of auto-dif in grad_log_prob_ad

Stan's set up to let you do (a) and make it fairly easy
to do (b).  I was referring to (b), which lets you accelerate
subcomputations by unfolding their auto-difs by hand.

To define a new function with hand-coded partials and
propagation, just follow the examples that are already in
Stan.  I go over an example in a bit more detail below.

>
>         One intermediate option that might make sense to think about is
>         having
>         a more limited code gen facility that could play well with the AD
>         facility that's currently in place. We could use that to
>         automatically
>         generate, e.g., log-probability vari classes that implement the
>         appropriate chain() functions. E.g., we could generate a
>         normal_vvv_vari class, a normal_vvd_vari class, etc., each of which
>         does all of the work that is currently being done by calling the
>         square(), operator/, operator-, operator*, log() functions and their
>         corresponding chain() functions. These normal_xxx_vari's would
>         produce
>         the same results, but with dramatically fewer calls to the various
>         chain() functions.
>
>         Just a thought.
>
>
>     Absolutely!
>
>     That's what I've been saying from the get go.  The best place
>     for optimization in the current design is in partial evaluation
>     of the derivatives in the log probability functions and in the
>     matrix functions.
>
>     We could also help with numerical stability by adding more
>     basic distributions expressed directly in terms of the unconstrained
>     parameters, like
>
>        bernoulli_logit(x|alpha) =def= bernoulli(x|ilogit(alpha))
>
>
>
> So the idea would be to implement a template specialization of, e.g.,
> normal_log, where the returned var points to one of these
> normal_xxx_vari's?  I might give that a shot for the multi_normal case.

Pretty much.  Except you need eight template specializations
if you want to catch accelerate all the ways in normal_log may
be called.

stan::agrad::var is just a pointer to the implementation,
which needs to be of type stan::agrad::vari.   stan::agrad::var
is designed with normal copy syntax.

It gets tricky with multiple arguments as you need to consider all
the possible double/var instantiations.  For instance,
here's the code to extend std::pow(double,double) to handle
auto-difs.  Custom user functions would be handled the same
way.

namespace stan {

   namespace agrad {

   namespace {  // makes classes anonymous

       class pow_vv_vari : public op_vv_vari {
       public:
         pow_vv_vari(vari* avi, vari* bvi) :
           op_vv_vari(std::pow(avi->val_,bvi->val_),avi,bvi) {
         }
         void chain() {
           if (avi_->val_ == 0.0) return;
           avi_->adj_ += adj_ * bvi_->val_ * val_ / avi_->val_;
           bvi_->adj_ += adj_ * std::log(avi_->val_) * val_;
         }
       };

       class pow_vd_vari : public op_vd_vari {
       public:
         pow_vd_vari(vari* avi, double b) :
           op_vd_vari(std::pow(avi->val_,b),avi,b) {
         }
         void chain() {
           if (avi_->val_ == 0.0) return;
           avi_->adj_ += adj_ * bd_ * val_ / avi_->val_;
         }
       };

       class pow_dv_vari : public op_dv_vari {
       public:
         pow_dv_vari(double a, vari* bvi) :
           op_dv_vari(std::pow(a,bvi->val_),a,bvi) {
         }
         void chain() {
           if (adj_ == 0.0) return;
           bvi_->adj_ += adj_ * std::log(ad_) * val_;
         }
       };
    }

    inline var pow(const var& base, const var& exponent) {
       return var(new pow_vv_vari(base.vi_,exponent.vi_));
     }

    inline var pow(const var& base, const double exponent) {
       if (exponent == 0.5)
         return sqrt(base);
       if (exponent == 1.0)
         return base;
       if (exponent == 2.0)
         return base * base;
       return var(new pow_vd_vari(base.vi_,exponent));
     }

     inline var pow(const double base, const var& exponent) {
       return var(new pow_dv_vari(base,exponent.vi_));
     }

   }
}

It's a lot of code, but it's all pretty simple and organized
consistently by convention.  You get
three function definitions that take arguments pow(var,var),
pow(var,double) and pow(double,var).  Each of these has
a corresponding implementation class extending vari.

The implementation classes (a) compute the value, (b)
store the arguments by passing to the appropriate parent
class, and (c) implement the chain() method to
pass partial derivatives.  Note that in (b), the first
argument is stored by the superclass as avi_ if it is a
stan::agrad::var and as ad_ if it is a double.

It's important to note the adj_ * in all of these.  The chain()
method applies the chain rule and adj_ is the partial of the
root expression with respect to the current expression, and chain()
increments the adj_ value of subexpressions (it needs to increment here
rather than set because subexpressions can be reused).

The pow(var,double) adds some extra optimization to deal with
powers for which there are built-in functions or trivial answers.
I should really replace (base * base) with square(base).  It's
another unfolding I did because we had so many squares floating
around everywhere.  square(double) has just one argument, so
there's less to store.

- Bob

