Doing it automatically seems unlikely to be able to actually get efficient implementations.

Another way to do this would be to encourage people to use multivariate distributions in cases like this.  The only other piece would be to add specializations of, e.g., multi_normal_log to handle passing diagonal or scalar times identity for the covariance to make it as fast as possible.  The same strategy could be used to vectorize multi_normal by defining a different distribution, e.g., vec_multi_normal(matrix,vector,matrix).

Alternatively, we just implement vectorized versions of the distributions.  A trivial (non-optimized) version would be really easy to do which could be swapped out as people implemented better versions.

I would vote for the latter myself.

Cheers,
Marcus

