Certainly not directly.  I think Matt's point was that it might
be a good place to start thinking about optimizations.

> Another way to do this would be to encourage people to use
> multivariate distributions in cases like this.

The basic approach wouldn't be any more efficient.
Less so, in fact, because of wrappers.

 > The only other piece
> would be to add specializations of, e.g., multi_normal_log to handle
> passing diagonal or scalar times identity for the covariance to make
> it as fast as possible.  The same strategy could be used to vectorize
> multi_normal by defining a different distribution, e.g.,
> vec_multi_normal(matrix,vector,matrix).

This could work.  Ben's been itching to be smarter about
vectors.  It seems so close given how Eigen handles them.
Easy enough to do in specific cases, but I don't see how to
make the resulting expression templates play nicely with everything
else without a whole lot of work.  This is the same problem
we're having trying to extend Eigen to different arg types.

The multivariate with scalar reduces to the next suggestion...

> Alternatively, we just implement vectorized versions of the
> distributions.  A trivial (non-optimized) version would be really
> easy to do which could be swapped out as people implemented better
> versions.
>
> I would vote for the latter myself.

That's the route down which we started.  So far,
there's only the implementation for

   double normal_log(vector ys, double mu, double sigma);

Going back to the last point, we might want a vector
for mu, too, because it's often a regression,

    mu[n] = beta * x[n].

