On 3/27/12 9:05 PM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Tuesday, March 27, 2012 7:21:21 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
>     Now here's the real problem. In order to get the advantages
>
>     of both expression templates *and* partial evaluation of
>     derivatives, we'll have to
>
>     (a) rewrite Eigen to support mixed-type binary ops; they're
>     up for it, but I'm not sure we are
>
> Reminder that we almost hacked in (a) in the mixed_type branch. In particular, it should work for Array ops like

I'm not as optimistic as you are about how close
we got.  I'm pretty sure we need to either
(a) follow their lead in std::complex and hand-build
a bunch of cases, or (b) follow their suggestion in
the feature request and generalize the way traits
are specified for operations.

I don't think their current traits can handle the mixed
operations.  If they did, then I think they would've done
std::complex that way.

I don't know how far they've pushed std::complex
through things like solvers, but we'd need to do that
for our applications (at least most of them are
not mixed operations, though if we did a/B or B\a it
they could be).

I'm not 100% sure on any of this, though.  It's so
intricate that I find it rather daunting.

> ArrayXd x, u, v;
> // allocate u and v
>
> double c = ???;
> x = u + c * v;

I don't get this example.  Isn't ArrayXd specialized for double?

> It just doesn't work (yet) with matrix ops because we / I haven't figured out how to specialize the gebp_traits class to
> work like the various combinations of complex and double. Also, I don't understand the gebp stuff to begin with.

I'll repeat that I find it all rather daunting.

I do think the Eigen developers are willing to help out
if we're serious and we get stuck.  They were really
helpful when I got stuck the first time trying to provide
operator overrides everywhere.

>     (b) push the auto-dif partial evaluations through to the Eigen's
>     expression templates
>
> True, and this would be a good bit of work, but no one is going to start on (b) until (a) happens.

Indeed.  Getting both of these done would be
the best thing to do.  I think there'd be a fairly
decent audience for it, too, completely independently of
Stan.

- Bob

