On Thursday, March 29, 2012 10:37:20 AM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> I hear the Intel compiler produces faster code
> than g++ or clang++, so it'd be nice to see the speed.

It's complicated (and I didn't test run-time speed). It definitely can be said that it compiles slowly; it took over an hour to run through test-all with one core. The benchmarks I have seen often don't make it 100% clear if they are talking about using the Intel Math Kernel Library (MKL) standalone or MKL with the Intel C++ compiler. So, I never have been able to get a clear picture of how much of the gain to attribute to the compiler itself. FWIW, the next version of Eigen will supposedly support using the MKL backend for some operations.

> I think we should claim we tested the Intel compiler
> and it worked, not that we're planning on supporting it
> going forward.  At least until we get the compilation
> more under control in the context of R and across
> Win/Linux/Mac for g++ and clang++.  I just don't want to
> have to field questions about it.

OK, although in general I would say that stan should support whatever compilers are supported by the intersection of boost and Eigen.

Ben

