>     I hear the Intel compiler produces faster code
>     than g++ or clang++, so it'd be nice to see the speed.
>
>
> It's complicated (and I didn't test run-time speed). It definitely can be said that it compiles slowly; it took over an
> hour to run through test-all with one core. The benchmarks I have seen often don't make it 100% clear if they are
> talking about using the Intel Math Kernel Library (MKL) standalone or MKL with the Intel C++ compiler. So, I never have
> been able to get a clear picture of how much of the gain to attribute to the compiler itself. FWIW, the next version of
> Eigen will supposedly support using the MKL backend for some operations.
>
>     I think we should claim we tested the Intel compiler
>     and it worked, not that we're planning on supporting it
>     going forward. At least until we get the compilation
>     more under control in the context of R and across
>     Win/Linux/Mac for g++ and clang++. I just don't want to
>     have to field questions about it.
>
> OK, although in general I would say that stan should support whatever compilers are supported by the intersection of
> boost and Eigen.

Maybe we mean different things by "support".  Right now,
our support is at the level of "works on my machine".

Here's what the Eigen FAQ has to say about their compiler
support:

------------------------------
Compiler support

Eigen is standard C++98 and so should theoretically be compatible with any compliant compiler. Whenever we use some
non-standard feature, that is optional and can be disabled.
Eigen is being successfully used with the following compilers:

GCC, version 3.4 and newer. Very good performance with GCC 4.2 and newer.
MSVC (Visual Studio), 2005 and newer. Vectorization is enabled with 2008 and newer, which also gives good performance.
Intel C++ compiler. Very good performance.
LLVM/CLang++ (2.8 and newer).
MinGW, recent versions. Very good performance when using GCC 4.
QNX's QCC compiler.
--------------------------

If that's all you mean by support, then by all means.  Let's list
everything where we've been able to get it to compile.  Anyone want
to volunteer to try MinGW on Windows?

The same version of g++ behaves very differently on the different platforms,
and what workedin 4.8 doesn't seem to work in 4.9.  It's been driving
us nuts.  It even behaves differently in terms of being able to succeed
with compilation at different optimization levels on the same platform.

Right now, our situation with Windows is that MSVC can compile the
parser, but not the models, whereas a suitably binary-edited verison of
g++ can compile the models but not the parser, and then only at
optimization level 3.  The other optimization levels cause the compiler
to output an empty object file.

- Bob

